<article class="env-lemma" id="037Q">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/037Q">Lemma <span data-tag="037Q">10.47.13</span>.</a> Let $K/k$ be a field extension. Consider the subextension $K/k'/k$ consisting of elements separably algebraic over $k$. Then $K$ is geometrically irreducible over $k'$. If $K/k$ is a finitely generated field extension, then $[k' : k] < \infty $. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The first statement is immediate from Lemma <a href="/tag/0G33" data-tag="0G33">10.47.12</a> and the fact that elements separably algebraic over $k'$ are in $k'$ by the transitivity of separable algebraic extensions, see Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/09HB" data-tag="09HB">9.12.12</a>. If $K/k$ is finitely generated, then $k'$ is finite over $k$ by Fields, Lemma <a href="/tag/037J" data-tag="037J">9.26.11</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>